/**
\defgroup psdmat Positive semidefinite structures
\brief Structured symmetric positive semidefinite matrices with fixed rank, log pseudo-determinants, and derivatives to order 6.

This module describes positive semidefinite matrices of fixed rank and two structures of this kind in namespace `laplace::pdmat`. The functions of \ref laplace::pdmat::reduced_rank_psdmat "reduced_rank_psdmat" (header `<laplace/pdmat/reduced_rank.hpp>`) are marked `LAPLACE_HD` and run on the host and inside Kokkos kernels. Those of \ref laplace::pdmat::weighted_sum_psdmat "weighted_sum_psdmat" (header `<laplace/pdmat/weighted_sum.hpp>`, linked with `Laplace::dense`) run on the host only, as does `params_from_matrix()` for every structure. Over orders 0 to 6, `reduced_rank_psdmat` keeps its matrix and factor within 0.63 ulpN and its log pseudo-determinant within 3850 ulp on gcc/OpenMP and 2946 ulp on CUDA. `weighted_sum_psdmat` keeps its log pseudo-determinant within \f$2.19 \times 10^4\f$ ulp over the same orders.

## Concept and interface

A positive semidefinite structure maps \f$q\f$ unconstrained parameters \f$\theta \in \mathbb{R}^q\f$ to a symmetric positive semidefinite \f$p \times p\f$ matrix \f$M(\theta)\f$ whose rank \f$r\f$ does not depend on \f$\theta\f$. In place of \f$\log\det M\f$, the structure supplies the log pseudo-determinant \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$, defined as the logarithm of the product of the \f$r\f$ nonzero eigenvalues of \f$M\f$. Unlike the positive definite structures on \ref pdmat "the positive definite structures page", a positive semidefinite structure has no inverse, no Cholesky factor, and no positive-definite descriptor flags (`unit_diagonal`, `closed_inverse`, `closed_factor`, `has_density`).

The concept \ref laplace::pdmat::psdmat_structure "psdmat_structure" requires `dim`, `n_params()`, `scratch_size()`, `rank()`, `matrix()`, `log_pdet()`, the functions `matrix_d1` to `matrix_d6` returning `bool`, `log_pdet_d1` to `log_pdet_d6` returning `double`, and host-only `params_from_matrix(m_hat, theta)` mapping a packed target matrix to parameters inside the domain. It also requires two compile-time boolean descriptors: `constant_null_space` (`true` when the null space does not depend on \f$\theta\f$) and `supplies_null_space` (`true` when `null_space_basis()` exists; the concept requires `null_space_basis()` only then). `weighted_sum_psdmat` sets both to `true`, and `reduced_rank_psdmat` sets both to `false`. The concept checks nothing about where a function runs, so a host-only structure satisfies it. The CRTP base \ref laplace::pdmat::psdmat_orders "psdmat_orders\<S\>" turns `matrix_dk<K>` and `log_pdet_dk<K>` (\f$1 \le K \le 6\f$) into those twelve named functions, as \ref laplace::pdmat::pdmat_orders "pdmat_orders" does for positive definite structures.

Packed lower-triangular storage, sorted parameter index tuples, caller scratch buffers `w`, and the `false` return of structurally zero matrix derivatives follow \ref pdmat "the positive definite structures page". Scalar derivatives `log_pdet_dk<K>()` return `0.0` when identically zero.

## Reduced-rank structure

The structure \ref laplace::pdmat::reduced_rank_psdmat "reduced_rank_psdmat" represents \f$M(\theta) = \Lambda\Lambda^\top\f$ of rank \f$d\f$, with \f$\Lambda\f$ of size \f$p \times d\f$. The constructor `reduced_rank_psdmat(p, d)` sets the fields `dim` and `n_factors`. The loadings follow the section "Factor-analytic structure" of \ref pdmat "the positive definite structures page": \f$\Lambda\f$ is lower trapezoidal with diagonal entries \f$\Lambda_{jj} = e^{\theta}\f$, and \f$\theta\f$ holds the \f$q = pd - d(d - 1)/2\f$ loadings by rows. With the same loadings, \f$M\f$ equals the factor-analytic \f$M\f$ minus \f$\Psi\f$, which the tests check at seven shapes.

The structure also provides `factor()` and `factor_d1()` to `factor_d6()`, which are not part of the concept. They write \f$\Lambda\f$ and its derivatives as a dense \f$p \times d\f$ row-major array with zeros above the diagonal (not packed). Neither \f$M^+\f$ nor a basis of the null space is supplied.

The host-only `params_from_matrix()` extracts the \f$d\f$ leading eigenpairs of the target \f$\hat{M}\f$, scales each eigenvector by the square root of its floored eigenvalue (floored at \f$10^{-6}\f$ times the largest, or at 1 when none is positive), and reduces the resulting \f$p \times d\f$ matrix to canonical lower-trapezoidal form via the first \f$d\f$ columns of the Cholesky factor of \f$\Lambda\Lambda^\top\f$ (with pivots floored at \f$10^{-6}\f$ times the largest). On an exact `reduced_rank_psdmat` matrix (\f$p = 5, d = 2\f$), round-trip parameter error \f$\max_i \lvert \hat{\theta}_i - \theta_i \rvert\f$ is \f$1.7 \times 10^{-16}\f$ (gcc 14.2, OpenMP; test bound \f$10^{-12}\f$).

## Domain and special values

The precondition \f$1 \le d \le p\f$ is not checked. Both `matrix_dk<K>()` and `factor_dk<K>()` return `false` without touching the output buffer when the derivative is zero by structure. At \f$d = p\f$, `log_pdet_dk<K>()` returns `2.0` at order 1 along a diagonal loading and `0.0` along a free loading or at higher orders.

## Method

The functions `matrix()` and `matrix_dk<K>()` evaluate \f$\Lambda\Lambda^\top\f$ and its closed sparse derivatives. The derivative computed by `factor_dk<K>()` is nonzero only along one repeated loading. It equals 1 at order 1 for a free loading and \f$e^\theta\f$ at every order for a diagonal loading.

Because the nonzero eigenvalues of \f$\Lambda\Lambda^\top\f$ are those of the \f$d \times d\f$ matrix \f$C = \Lambda^\top\Lambda\f$, `log_pdet()` computes \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+ = \log\det C\f$. At \f$d = p\f$, \f$\Lambda\f$ is square and triangular, so \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+ = 2\sum_j \theta_{jj}\f$ holds directly, where \f$\theta_{jj}\f$ is the parameter of \f$\Lambda_{jj}\f$. For \f$d < p\f$, `log_pdet()` computes \f$\log\det C = 2\sum_k \log R_{kk}\f$ from a Cholesky factorization \f$C = R R^\top\f$.

The derivatives of \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$ for \f$d < p\f$ along a sorted tuple \f$F\f$ of \f$K\f$ parameter indices come from a recursion over the sub-tuples of \f$F\f$. For a sub-tuple \f$T\f$, write \f$C[T]\f$ for the mixed derivative of \f$C\f$ along \f$T\f$ and \f$X[T]\f$ for that of \f$X = C^{-1}\f$, with \f$X[\emptyset] = C^{-1}\f$. Then
\f[
X[S] = -X[\emptyset] \sum_{\emptyset \ne T \subseteq S} C[T]\, X[S \setminus T]
\f]
and, with \f$f\f$ the first index of \f$F\f$,
\f[
\partial^F \log\det C = \sum_{T \subseteq F \setminus \{f\}} \operatorname{tr}\!\big(X[T]\, C[\{f\} \cup (F \setminus \{f\} \setminus T)]\big).
\f]
Here \f$C[T]\f$ is zero unless every index of \f$T\f$ is a loading of one row \f$r\f$ of \f$\Lambda\f$; then it is the Leibniz sum, over the indices of \f$T\f$, of the derivatives of \f$\lambda_r\lambda_r^\top\f$, where \f$\lambda_r\f$ is row \f$r\f$ of \f$\Lambda\f$ written as a column.

## Accuracy

Error metrics are defined on \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured": errors of \f$M\f$ and \f$\Lambda\f$ are measured in normwise `ulpN`, and errors of \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$ in relative `ulp` of \f$\max(\lvert\mathrm{ref}\rvert, 1)\f$. References were generated in mpmath at 640 bits, computing \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$ from the \f$d\f$ largest eigenvalues of \f$M\f$ independently of \f$\Lambda^\top\Lambda\f$, with derivatives by mixed central differences.

Over orders 0 to 6, \f$M\f$ stays within 0.63 ulpN on gcc/OpenMP and 0.55 ulpN on CUDA (both at order 0), and \f$\Lambda\f$ stays within 0.49 ulpN on gcc and 0.43 ulpN on CUDA; the test bound is 1 ulpN at every order for both. At \f$d = p\f$, \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$ stays within 0.42 ulp at every order. Maximum measured errors and test bounds from `tests/pdmat/test_reduced_rank.cpp` for \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$ are:

| Order | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| gcc (ulp) | 0.97 | 9.6 | 131 | 742 | 161 | 3850 | 3682 |
| CUDA (ulp) | 1.6 | 21 | 146 | 219 | 1440 | 2502 | 2946 |
| Bound (ulp) | 3 | 31 | 220 | 1113 | 2161 | 5776 | 5524 |

The peaks at orders 4 to 6 come from the spread point of \f$(p, d) = (5, 4)\f$ along one repeated diagonal loading. There one ulp of \f$\theta\f$ moves the exact order-5 derivative by 1.1 ulp and the order-6 derivative by 2.2 ulp, so the loss is the recursion's, not the problem's: the Leibniz sums of a repeated \f$e^\theta\f$ grow like \f$2^K\f$ and cancel in the traces. For details, see `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

The times below were measured with gcc, per call, on one thread and with OpenMP on 8 threads.

| \f$(p, d)\f$ | `log_pdet()` | `log_pdet_d6()` |
|---|---|---|
| \f$(4, 1)\f$ | 127 ns, 23 ns | 1910 ns, 317 ns |
| \f$(16, 4)\f$ | 2223 ns, 299 ns | 9686 ns, 2171 ns |

## Parallel evaluation in Kokkos

The following kernel allocates one scratch row per work item from a two-dimensional `Kokkos::View` sized by `scratch_size()`, and evaluates \f$M\f$, \f$\log\lvert M\rvert_+\f$, and its first partial derivative:

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/pdmat/core.hpp>
#include <laplace/pdmat/reduced_rank.hpp>

void eval_psdmat(const Kokkos::View<double* [7]>& theta,
                 const Kokkos::View<double*>& out_lp,
                 const Kokkos::View<double*>& out_dlp) {
  constexpr int p = 4, d = 2, q = p * d - d * (d - 1) / 2;
  const laplace::pdmat::reduced_rank_psdmat s(p, d);
  Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutRight> scratch("scratch", theta.extent(0), s.scratch_size());
  Kokkos::parallel_for(
      "eval_psdmat", theta.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(int i) {
        double th[q];
        for (int k = 0; k < q; ++k) th[k] = theta(i, k);
        double m[laplace::pdmat::packed_size(p)];
        double* w = &scratch(i, 0);
        s.matrix(th, m, w);
        out_lp(i) = s.log_pdet(th, w);
        const int idx[1] = {0};
        out_dlp(i) = s.log_pdet_d1(th, idx, w);
      });
}
```

## Weighted sums of penalties

The structure \ref laplace::pdmat::weighted_sum_psdmat "weighted_sum_psdmat" maps \f$q = m\f$ unconstrained parameters \f$\theta \in \mathbb{R}^m\f$ to
\f[
M(\theta) = \sum_{j=1}^{m} e^{\theta_j} S_j,
\f]
where \f$S_1, \dots, S_m\f$ are fixed symmetric positive semidefinite \f$p \times p\f$ matrices and \f$\lambda_j = e^{\theta_j}\f$. The functions run on the host and call \ref dense "the dense module". The constructor copies a `std::vector` of host Views of the \f$S_j\f$ and throws `std::invalid_argument` when a matrix is not positive semidefinite or the sizes disagree.

The null space of a sum of positive semidefinite matrices is the intersection of theirs, so the rank \f$r\f$ and the null space do not depend on \f$\theta\f$. Both are computed once, at construction, and returned by `rank()` and `null_space_basis()`; no pseudo-inverse is supplied. The derivatives of \f$M\f$ are \f$e^{\theta_j}S_j\f$ along a tuple that repeats one index \f$j\f$ and zero by structure otherwise.

The host-only `params_from_matrix()` estimates positive weights \f$\lambda_j\f$ by least squares in the Frobenius inner product (solving the normal equations with Gram matrix \f$\langle S_j, S_k \rangle_F\f$), floors \f$\lambda_j \max \lvert S_j \rvert\f$ at \f$10^{-6}\f$ times the largest (setting \f$\theta_j = 0\f$ when none is positive), and sets \f$\theta_j = \log \lambda_j\f$. On an exact three-term weighted sum of \f$6 \times 6\f$ difference penalties, round-trip parameter error \f$\max_i \lvert \hat{\theta}_i - \theta_i \rvert\f$ is \f$2.1 \times 10^{-13}\f$ (gcc 14.2, OpenMP; test bound \f$10^{-12}\f$; three digits are lost to the conditioning of the \f$3 \times 3\f$ Gram matrix).
### Method

When the \f$\lambda_j\f$ spread apart, an eigendecomposition of \f$M\f$ in double resolves the small eigenvalues only to \f$\lambda_{\max}\epsilon\f$, and the derivatives lose one digit per order of magnitude of the spread. Instead, `log_pdet()` and its derivatives use a graded similarity transform after Wood (2011, appendix B), which groups the components by \f$\lambda_j\lVert S_j\rVert\f$, with \f$\lVert S\rVert\f$ the largest absolute entry. Let \f$U\f$ be an orthonormal basis of the subspace not yet resolved, \f$I_p\f$ at first. Each level:

1. drops the components whose restriction \f$R_j = U^\top S_j U\f$ has \f$\lVert R_j\rVert \le\f$ `zero_tolerance` \f$\lVert S_j\rVert\f$;
2. takes as dominant those whose \f$\theta_j + \log\lVert R_j\rVert\f$ is within \f$\log\f$ `split_tolerance` of the largest such value \f$t\f$;
3. splits \f$U\f$ by the eigendecomposition of \f$\sum_{\mathrm{dom}} R_j / \lVert R_j\rVert\f$ into its range \f$U_+\f$ and null space \f$U_0\f$, with the rank rule of the dense module;
4. appends \f$U U_+\f$ to \f$Q\f$ with the scale \f$d_l = e^t\f$ and \f$r_l\f$ columns;
5. continues on \f$U U_0\f$, where the dominant components are zero.

These structural zeros are set, never computed: a zero computed in floating point would carry an error of order \f$\lambda_{\mathrm{dom}}\epsilon\f$, with \f$\lambda_{\mathrm{dom}}\f$ the weight of a dominant component, and swamp the subordinate terms. Let \f$D\f$ be the diagonal matrix of the scales, each \f$d_l\f$ repeated \f$r_l\f$ times. Let \f$G_j = D^{-1/2} Q^\top (\lambda_j S_j) Q D^{-1/2}\f$ with the zeros set, and \f$H = \sum_j G_j\f$, whose entries are of order one. Then
\f[
\log\lvert M\rvert_+ = \log\det H + \sum_l r_l \log d_l,
\f]
with \f$\log\det H\f$ from Cholesky.

The matrix \f$B_j = H^{-1} G_j\f$ is similar to \f$\lambda_j M^+ S_j\f$ on the range of \f$M\f$, where \f$M^+\f$ is the pseudo-inverse. Hence \f$\partial\log\lvert M\rvert_+/\partial\theta_j = \operatorname{tr} B_j\f$ and \f$\partial B_j/\partial\theta_k = \delta_{jk}B_j - B_k B_j\f$. Along a sorted tuple of \f$K\f$ positions, the derivative is a sum over the set partitions \f$\pi\f$ of the positions whose blocks each repeat one index (Comtet, 1974). Each partition contributes \f$(-1)^{\lvert\pi\rvert - 1}\f$ times the sum over the \f$(\lvert\pi\rvert - 1)!\f$ cyclic block orders of \f$\operatorname{tr}(B_{j_1}\cdots B_{j_{\lvert\pi\rvert}})\f$, where \f$j_b\f$ is the index of block \f$b\f$. In exact arithmetic \f$\sum_{j=1}^m \operatorname{tr} B_j = r\f$, which `rank_identity()` returns as a check. The default `split_tolerance`, \f$\epsilon^{0.05} \approx 0.165\f$, was the best measured: a level holds the components within a factor of about 6 of the largest. The default `zero_tolerance` is \f$8p\epsilon\f$.

### Accuracy

Errors are in ulp of \f$\max(\lvert\mathrm{ref}\rvert, 1)\f$, as on \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". The references come from mpmath (2023) at 256 bits on an exact rational basis of the range from SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017), with spreads of the \f$\lambda_j\f$ up to \f$10^{20}\f$. The table gives the worst gcc errors and test bounds on two tensor-product cases, of sizes 30 and 36. The CUDA build gives the same errors.

| Order | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Worst, tensor-product cases (ulp) | 1.96 | 0.89 | 48.5 | 92 | 253 | 2100 | \f$1.73 \times 10^4\f$ |
| Test bound, tensor-product cases (ulp) | 8 | 4 | 200 | 400 | 1000 | 8500 | 70000 |

The loss that grows with the order comes from the alternating signs of the partition sum. A test case with one component of condition number \f$2^{16}\f$ loses \f$1.1 \times 10^4\f$ to \f$2.19 \times 10^4\f$ ulp at every order, also at equal \f$\lambda_j\f$: the loss comes from that component. The rank identity holds within \f$6.1 \times 10^{-14}\f$. See `docs/numerics.md`.

### Speed

The per-call functions recompute the transform on each call. On one gcc thread, `log_pdet()` takes 8.2 microseconds at \f$p = 8\f$ and 218 at \f$p = 36\f$; `log_pdet_d6()` takes 55.9 and 718.

When evaluating a table of derivatives at one \f$\theta\f$, compute the state once with \ref laplace::pdmat::weighted_sum_psdmat::log_pdet_state "log_pdet_state()" and read every derivative from the same scratch buffer `w` with \ref laplace::pdmat::weighted_sum_psdmat::log_pdet_dk_at "log_pdet_dk_at()". This uses no extra memory and returns the same values bit for bit as the per-call functions. Measurements on one gcc thread cover tables of every sorted tuple of orders 0 to \f$K\f$, for \f$m\f$ penalties splitting the second-difference penalty of \f$p\f$ columns. Up to order 2, the gain ranges from 5.4 at \f$m = 2\f$, \f$p = 10\f$ to 15 at \f$m = 4\f$, \f$p = 20\f$, because the transform is the whole cost. Up to order 6, the gain is 1.4 at \f$m = 2\f$, \f$p = 10\f$ to 4.5 at \f$m = 2\f$, \f$p = 50\f$, because the partition sum over word traces dominates.

### Host evaluation

A host example with a second-difference penalty and the identity:

```cpp
#include <laplace/pdmat/weighted_sum.hpp>
#include <cstdio>
#include <vector>

void eval_weighted_sum() {  // inside a Kokkos::ScopeGuard
  using laplace::pdmat::weighted_sum_psdmat;
  weighted_sum_psdmat::host_matrix d2("d2", 3, 3), id("id", 3, 3);
  const double a[3] = {1.0, -2.0, 1.0};
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) d2(i, k) = a[i] * a[k];
    id(i, i) = 1.0;
  }
  const weighted_sum_psdmat s(std::vector{d2, id});
  std::vector<double> w(s.scratch_size());
  const double theta[2] = {1.0, -2.0};
  const int idx[2] = {0, 1};
  std::printf("rank %d, log pdet %g, d2 %g\n", s.rank(), s.log_pdet(theta, w.data()),
              s.log_pdet_d2(theta, idx, w.data()));
}
```

## Public classes and concepts

- \ref laplace::pdmat::psdmat_structure "psdmat_structure": Concept defining structured positive semidefinite matrix requirements.
- \ref laplace::pdmat::psdmat_orders "psdmat_orders\<S\>": CRTP base providing named derivative member functions.
- \ref laplace::pdmat::reduced_rank_psdmat "reduced_rank_psdmat": Reduced-rank positive semidefinite matrix structure \f$\Lambda\Lambda^\top\f$.
- \ref laplace::pdmat::weighted_sum_psdmat "weighted_sum_psdmat": Host-only weighted sum \f$\sum_{j=1}^{m} e^{\theta_j} S_j\f$.

## References

- L. Comtet (1974), Advanced Combinatorics: The Art of Finite and Infinite Expansions, Reidel. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-010-2196-8
- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- S. N. Wood (2011), Fast Stable Restricted Maximum Likelihood and Marginal Likelihood Estimation of Semiparametric Generalized Linear Models, Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology, 73(1), 3–36. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2010.00749.x

*/
